// @check
// @error operator "[]" for i32[] can never dispatch: indexing i32[] is the language's own, never a user operator's
// @error operator "[]=" for string can never dispatch: indexing string is the language's own, never a user operator's
// @error operator "[]" for Xs can never dispatch: indexing i32[] is the language's own, never a user operator's
// @error operator "[]" for Bs can never dispatch: indexing Bs is the language's own, never a user operator's
// @error operator "[]" for {[string]:i32} can never dispatch: indexing {[string]: i32} is the language's own, never a user operator's
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D445 — the DECLARATION side of the rule
// `operator-builtin-unaffected.vl` pins from the USE side.
//
// THE SWALLOW WAS ALREADY A CONTRACT; IT WAS JUST SILENT AT THE DECLARATION.
// `checkIndexNode` asks `tyBuiltinIndexable` and walks past every `"[]"`
// declaration for an array, a map or a string, and the sibling fixture asserts it
// deliberately: "declaring index operators does not change how the LANGUAGE's own
// indexable types behave". So `function "[]"(self: i32[], i: i32) { return 99 }` +
// `print(xs[0])` was `vl check` rc 0 and printed `1` — not a dispatch that failed,
// a dispatch the language had already decided would never happen. Nothing said so.
//
// KEYED ON THE RESOLVED TYPE, which is why it cannot live beside the parser's
// `indexOpDeclName` where the arity twin's spelling-only cousin lives: an ALIAS
// (`Xs`) and a NEWTYPE (`Bs`) are both swallowed and neither is spelled `i32[]`.
// The gate routes through `tyBuiltinIndexable` itself rather than re-listing its
// arms, so the reject and the swallow cannot drift apart.
//
// THE TWO ARMS IT SUBTRACTS are the ones that function answers `true` for out of
// caution rather than knowledge: an un-pinned hole and the error type. A hole is a
// LIVE receiver — `function "[]"<T>(self: T, i: i32)` accepts and dispatches for
// every non-built-in receiver — and an error type has already been reported at the
// annotation, so refusing either would be a false reject on a reported line.
// (`operator-unannotated-self.vl` refuses the un-ANNOTATED `self` separately, at
// the parser, for a different reason: the name cannot be minted.)
//
// STD IS THE CUSTOMER THAT AGREES WITH IT. `std/buffer.vl` exports four index
// operators over `F32View`/`I32View`, which are `new { base, length }` — nominal
// STRUCTS, never built-in indexable — and they dispatch correctly today (the
// emitted module calls the `"[]"` function, which tail-calls `getI32`). They are
// untouched by this reject, as is every one of the 45 index-operator declarations
// in the tree: not one has a built-in indexable receiver.
type Bx = { v: i32 }
type Xs = i32[]
type Bs = new i32[]

function "[]"(self: i32[], i: i32): i32 { return 99 }

function "[]="(self: string, i: i32, v: i32) { print(99) }

function "[]"(self: Xs, i: i32): i32 { return 99 }

function "[]"(self: Bs, i: i32): i32 { return 99 }

function "[]"(self: {[string]: i32}, k: string): i32 { return 99 }

// CONTROL — a STRUCT receiver is not the language's to index, so it dispatches and
// must keep dispatching. This is std's shape.
function "[]"(self: Bx, i: i32): i32 { return self.v + i }

const b: Bx = { v: 10 }
print(b[1])
